WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.315
Many Go teams use an ORM, a query builder,
generated query code,

2
00:00:03.395 --> 00:00:06.475
or stored procedures instead of writing
every SQL statement by hand.

3
00:00:06.555 --> 00:00:10.378
These tools can reduce repetitive string
handling and can make safer patterns easier

4
00:00:10.458 --> 00:00:10.936
to follow.

5
00:00:11.016 --> 00:00:12.715
They are still tools, not guarantees.

6
00:00:12.795 --> 00:00:16.619
Developers need to understand how each
abstraction separates values from structure

7
00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:19.301
and where the abstraction exposes
lower-level features.

8
00:00:19.381 --> 00:00:21.664
An ORM may parameterize common lookups by
default,

9
00:00:21.744 --> 00:00:24.559
but it may also provide raw query
functions for advanced cases.

10
00:00:24.639 --> 00:00:27.506
A query builder may keep arguments
separate when used normally,

11
00:00:27.586 --> 00:00:29.339
but it may also allow custom fragments.

12
00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:32.286
Generated query code can make fixed query
shapes easy to review,

13
00:00:32.366 --> 00:00:35.871
but the generated layer still depends on
how query definitions are written and

14
00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:36.535
maintained.

15
00:00:36.615 --> 00:00:40.439
The safest use of these abstractions keeps
the same principle as direct database/sql

16
00:00:40.519 --> 00:00:40.784
code.

17
00:00:40.864 --> 00:00:42.936
Runtime values belong in argument
positions.

18
00:00:43.016 --> 00:00:45.299
Dynamic structure should come from known
options.

19
00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:47.450
Raw SQL entry points should be rare,
reviewed,

20
00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.194
and documented so the team understands why
the lower-level path was necessary and

21
00:00:51.274 --> 00:00:52.443
how values are protected.

22
00:00:52.523 --> 00:00:56.346
Stored procedures can centralize database
behavior and reduce repeated query logic

23
00:00:56.426 --> 00:00:57.382
in application code.

24
00:00:57.462 --> 00:01:01.126
They can also hide unsafe string assembly
inside the database layer if teams treat

25
00:01:01.206 --> 00:01:02.481
them as automatically safe.

26
00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:05.003
Review should include the procedure
implementation,

27
00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:08.270
the permissions used to run it, and the
way application values reach it.

28
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.589
The safest abstraction is the one the team
understands well enough to use

29
00:01:11.669 --> 00:01:12.359
consistently.

30
00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:16.263
Conventions, examples, linting, and code
review should guide developers toward the

31
00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:16.874
safer path.

32
00:01:16.954 --> 00:01:19.343
When a library offers several ways to
express a query,

33
00:01:19.423 --> 00:01:22.928
the team should document which patterns
are preferred and which require extra

34
00:01:23.008 --> 00:01:23.380
review.
